    dict(id='read-sp2', course='SP', title='The developing athlete, 10 to 25', covers=['SP2'], name='SP2 · The developing athlete, 10 to 25', intro='The course\'s second unit. It sets out the three clocks a coach reads in every athlete (chronological, biological and training age); the timing of puberty and what it does to performance, selection and self-image; relative age effects; the brain from adolescence into the twenties, and the claim that it "finishes at 25"; reward, peers and evaluation, and what does not follow from a brain image; self-regulation and emotion regulation across four age bands (about 10 to 12, 13 to 15, 16 to 18, 19 to 25); identity from 16 to 25, the junior-to-senior transition and the end of a career; sleep need by age; early specialisation, sampling and the 10,000 hours; and how weakly early success predicts adult success. Its deliverable is an age-and-stage table, every cell graded, and a development note worked on the three fictional athletes and then given as a template. How it was made: written on 17 Nov 2026 by Opus 5.5 with web search off, then checked by a separate Opus 5.5 context that had not seen the writing (verdict: fix; 0 errors, 10 should-fix, 8 minor). The same day it was corrected in two rounds, each change checked against its source by a fresh Opus 5.5 checker before it was applied (24 of 29 checks passed in the first round, 4 of 5 in the second), and given five quality reviews by fresh Opus 5.5 agents with live search, every verdict fix: the first found 0 errors, 7 should-fix and 8 minor, all applied; the last found 0 errors, 4 should-fix and 2 minor. A cross-unit check of SP1 to SP3 by a fresh Opus 5.5 agent found 10 disagreements; the one in this unit was corrected, and two that would change the grade key itself are left to a syllabus review. On 18 Nov 2026 (row W-SP4) it was corrected again: a corrections check by a fresh Opus 5.5 agent with live search passed 7 of 9 changes, and a sixth quality review by a fresh Opus 5.5 agent found 0 errors, 4 should-fix and 8 minor; 7 of its findings were applied after a fresh agent checked each against its source, and 5, on Cobley et al. (2009) and Boccia et al. (2017), were held because the checking agents could not open their DOI addresses. Known gaps (checked 18 Nov 2026). (1) References not opened, on which no claim rests: the review of maturation in youth athletes (Malina et al., 2015); Somerville (2016) and Sawyer et al. (2018), on the age of brain maturity, so the 25 claim is said not to hold for the measures opened and is left unsettled for others; the review of 126 studies on the end of a career, reported only as the ISSP position stand gives it; the athlete sleep consensus (Walsh et al., 2021); the athletics study of career trajectories from youth to senior (Boccia et al., 2017) and its published correction, so the advice to record each athlete\'s rate of improvement carries no grade from it; the other studies in the bibliography of the emotion-regulation study; the studies behind the NSCA\'s eight-month limit and the ISSP\'s career advice, graded practice on the consensus alone; the reference behind the IOC\'s 8.5 to 9.5 hours of sleep (its ref. 43); and the growth studies behind the IOC\'s summary that girls often outscore boys on cycling peak power until about 12 (its ref. 30), graded weak with the label inference. (2) Claims read from an abstract or summary only: the maturation meta-analysis (Albaladejo-Saura et al., 2021); the review of adolescent brain maturation (Arain et al., 2013); Foulkes and Blakemore (2018); the relative-age meta-analysis in sport overall (Cobley et al., 2009), whose abstract is itself not verified by an independent check, because its DOI address could not be fetched, so it carries no grade, and in female sport (Smith et al., 2018); the multidisciplinary-practice meta-analysis (Güllich et al., 2022). Read in full on 18 Nov 2026: Güllich et al. (2023), at the publisher\'s page, with the 7.2 and 92.8 per cent figures from its abstract; Macnamara et al. (2016) and Crone and Dahl (2012), on copies of the published PDFs, not the publishers\' pages. The moderate grades that rest on the abstract-only meta-analyses were set without their intervals, sensitivity analyses or study quality. (3) Not verified: the last page of the IOC statement (Bergeron et al., 2015), as printed on a copy; the authors, volume, issue and pages of Cobley et al. (2009); the authors, volume and issue of Boccia et al. (2017); the issue of Crone and Dahl (2012); the authors, volume, pages and DOI of Güllich et al. (2022); the DOI of the NSCA statement, not printed on the page read; the volume of Lorenz and Kray (2022), whose issue and pages come from an index record; the authors, journal, volume and issue of the ISSP position stand, not shown on the page read. The short AASM sleep statement (Paruthi et al., 2016) was checked on its PubMed record and its content read in the methodology paper; the adult statement (Watson et al., 2015) was read in its dual-published version, its record in Sleep not opened. (4) Unsourced: self-image and body image at puberty (the mood evidence is one meta-analysis in girls and women, not athletes), and emotion regulation from 16 to 25; both are stated in the unit as unsourced. The size of the relative-age effect in male sport is not given, as the abstract read gives no figure.'),
